Using Redis is a common way to prevent duplicate bookings in a distributed system where multiple application instances are running. The idea is to use a distributed lock so that only one instance can attempt to book a specific table at a time.

However, it's important to understand that Redis alone is not the source of truth. The database remains the source of truth, and Redis is used to coordinate concurrent access.

                Load Balancer
                      |
        -----------------------------
        |             |             |
     App-1         App-2         App-3
        |             |             |
        ---------------|-------------
                       |
                    Redis Lock
                       |
                    Database

Suppose two users try to book at exactly the same time.

Table-10 -> 7 PM - 9 PM

Step 1: Generate Lock Key
        Use a unique key representing the resource being booked -> lock:table:10:2026-07-25:19:00
Step 2: Acquire Lock
        Redis provides an atomic operation -> SET lock:table:10 UUID NX PX 30000
        where
        NX → Set only if key doesn't already exist
        PX 30000 → Lock expires after 30 seconds
        UUID → Unique owner identifier

Example:-
       Thread A -> SET lock:table:10 abc123 NX PX 30000 => Result OK   => Thread A owns the lock.
       Thread A -> SET lock:table:10 xyz789 NX PX 30000 => Result null => Lock already exists.

       Thread B can either:
        1. Retry after a short delay
        2. Return "Booking in progress"
        3. Queue the request
